From: PS3 Linux project <ps3@localhost>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 05:33:33 +0300
Subject: Sockets non-blocking on big-endian 64-bit too (FIONBIO takes an int)

net_set_blocking_impl() (net_set_non_blocking, net_set_blocking) gave ioctl(FIONBIO) a pointer
to an unsigned long. Linux reads an int there (sock_ioctl: get_user on an int *). On
little-endian the int is the long's low half and holds the 1; on big-endian 64-bit (ppc64) it is
the high half, 0, so the UDP sockets of the client and the server stayed blocking. Same bug as
Teeworlds 0.7.5's (teeworlds/ patch 0001): the client sits in recvfrom() after the server's
accept and shows the last loading frame.

Windows keeps the unsigned long (ioctlsocket takes a u_long *).

diff --git a/src/base/net.cpp b/src/base/net.cpp
--- a/src/base/net.cpp
+++ b/src/base/net.cpp
@@ -688,7 +688,12 @@ int net_socket_type(NETSOCKET sock)
 
 static int net_set_blocking_impl(NETSOCKET sock, bool blocking)
 {
+#if defined(CONF_FAMILY_WINDOWS)
 	unsigned long mode = blocking ? 0 : 1;
+#else
+	// FIONBIO takes an int on Linux: an unsigned long read as an int is its high half (0) on big-endian 64-bit
+	int mode = blocking ? 0 : 1;
+#endif
 	const char *mode_str = blocking ? "blocking" : "non-blocking";
 	int sockets[] = {sock->ipv4sock, sock->ipv6sock};
 	const char *socket_str[] = {"IPv4", "IPv6"};
